<Base title="Matrix LLM: privacy policy" description="What the Matrix LLM app for iPhone, iPad, Mac and Windows stores, where, and when anything leaves your device.">
  <article class="policy">
  <p class="meta"><a href="/projects/matrix/">Matrix LLM</a> · last updated {updated}</p>
  <h1>Privacy policy: Matrix LLM</h1>
  <p class="lede">Matrix LLM works on your own device. I, the developer, receive nothing from the app: no documents,
  no questions, no usage data, no crash reports. There is no account, no telemetry, no tracking and no advertising.</p>

  <p>This policy covers the Matrix LLM app on iPhone, iPad, Mac and Windows. The Matrix plugin for Claude Code has <a href="https://github.com/matthiasnordwig/matrix-mcp/blob/main/PRIVACY.md">its own privacy policy</a>.</p>

  <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
  <p>Matthias Nordwig, <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>. The postal address is in the <a href="/impressum/">legal notice</a>.</p>

  <h2>What the app stores, and where</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Your collections: the documents you add, the passages cut from them, the search index, answers, tables and
    knowledge graphs.</li>
    <li>Your settings, including the addresses and keys of model services you add yourself.</li>
    <li>Downloaded models: the search model, optionally a language model, and on Windows the text recognition models.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>All of it stays in the app's data folder on your device. The app has no cloud sync of its own; a device backup
  you set up yourself, such as iCloud Backup or Time Machine, may include the folder.</p>
  <p>Keys for model services are stored encrypted in the app's database. The key that decrypts them is a file in the
  same folder, so this protects a copy of the database on its own, not the whole folder.</p>

  <h2>When data leaves your device</h2>
  <p>Only in the cases below. Almost all of them start with something you do; the text recognition download on Windows
  and images in answers do not ask separately.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Model downloads from Hugging Face.</strong> On first start the app asks before it downloads its search
    model and, if you want one, a language model from huggingface.co. If you search for other models in the app, your
    search words go to Hugging Face. No document content, account or token is sent. Hugging Face sees the request and
    your IP address, under <a href="https://huggingface.co/privacy">its privacy policy</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Text recognition models on Windows.</strong> The first time the Windows app reads text from an image or
    a scanned page, it downloads three small text recognition files (about 13 MB) from huggingface.co, without asking
    separately. Only the download request is sent. On iPhone, iPad and Mac the app uses Apple's built-in text recognition
    and downloads nothing for it.</li>
    <li><strong>Model services you add.</strong> If you connect a service such as OpenAI, Azure OpenAI, Anthropic,
    Google Gemini, another OpenAI-compatible service, Ollama or a server of your own, the app sends that service what
    the task needs: your question and the passages found for it; for tables, summaries and the knowledge graph, the text
    of the passages being processed; page images if you use a model that reads images. A search model or reranker that
    runs at a service receives the text it has to process, for a search model all text of the documents you index. Each
    key is sent only to the service you entered it for. What happens there is governed by that service's privacy policy.</li>
    <li><strong>Apple Intelligence.</strong> Apple's model runs on your device. Where Apple offers Private Cloud Compute
    and you choose that option, requests are processed on Apple's servers under Apple's terms.</li>
    <li><strong>Access for AI agents (Mac and Windows, off by default).</strong> If you turn it on, programs on the same
    computer that have its access key can use your collections: by default they can search and read them; adding files
    or changing graphs and tables needs further switches. It listens only on your own computer. An extra option, also
    off by default, opens it to devices on your local network: only private network addresses are accepted and the
    access key is still required, but the connection is not encrypted, so use it only on a network you trust.</li>
    <li><strong>Claude Code as the app's model.</strong> If you connect Claude Code (or another agent) as a model, the
    app's model requests, with their passages, go to that agent and from there to the model service it uses, under your
    own account with that service.</li>
    <li><strong>Links and images.</strong> Links in the app, including links in a model's answer, open in your browser.
    If a model writes an image address from the web into an answer, the app may load that image when it shows the
    answer.</li>
    <li><strong>Sharing and export.</strong> Files leave the app when you share or save them yourself.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Nothing else is sent. The app has no update check of its own; updates come through the App Store or Microsoft Store.</p>

  <h2>AI-generated answers and reporting them</h2>
  <p>Answers, summaries, tables and graph relations are written by a language model and can be wrong. Every answer
  shows the passages it is based on. Matrix provides no models of its own; it runs the model you chose. To report an
  inappropriate or harmful answer, use <em>Report answer</em> next to it. It names the model and its provider and opens
  the provider's report page in your browser; nothing is sent until you submit a report there. Matrix itself sends
  nothing, also not to me. The reported answer is hidden on your device and can be shown again.</p>

  <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
  <p>Deleting a collection in the app deletes its data. Removing the app from an iPhone or iPad, or uninstalling the
  Microsoft Store version on Windows, removes the data folder. On a Mac, moving the app to the Trash does not: its data
  stays in <code>~/Library/Containers/de.programmiere.matrixllm</code> until you delete that folder.</p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>Because the app sends me nothing, I hold no data about you from it that I could show, correct or delete. Data
  held by a service you connected is a matter for that service. Questions go to the address above.</p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>The app is not directed at children and collects no personal data from anyone.</p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>Changes to this policy are published at this address with a new date.</p>
  </article>
</Base>

